    "deadlineDetails": "[2027 confirmed] December 15, 2026 for Fall 2027, for both US and non-US applicants. The program also publishes December 15, 2027 for Fall 2028. A cutoff time and time zone are not stated. The department may accept late applications, but full consideration is not assured; do not plan around late acceptance. [s1] [s2]",
    "funding": "[Conflict] The mathematics prospective-student page reports a Fall 2026 TA stipend of USD 40,000 for ten months, with staff health benefits; it describes fellowship stipends of USD 30,000-35,000 with different insurance. These are not verified Fall 2027 offers. An older mathematics page still quotes USD 25,969 for nine months from 2013 and must not be used as a current rate. The October 2025 SGS handbook gives an internal fellowship floor of USD 30,833 per academic year or USD 37,000 per calendar year, requiring reconciliation with the department’s lower fellowship figure. Tuition is normally covered; exact fees, insurance contributions, taxes and summer funding depend on the offer. [s9] [s10] [s12] [s13]",
    "fundingType": "Unknown",
    "overview": "[Standing rule] An international applicant may apply to the full-time, fall-entry PhD with a completed bachelor’s degree and preparation comparable to an honors mathematics major. A mathematics degree is not formally required. A UChicago mathematics bachelor’s is eligible in principle if completed by enrollment; admission remains competitive and F1 status is not itself an exclusion. [s1] [s2] [s3]",
    "admissions": "[Standing rule] Select Mathematics, PhD, New Brunswick, Fall 2027 in the central application; create a CommunityID; prepare transcripts, statement, CV and referee details. Ask referees before completing their section, which triggers invitations. Select financial-aid consideration if wanted. Arrange English-waiver evidence and resolve any checklist requirement with graduate admissions. Apply through the central portal rather than sending an application to the department. [s1] [s2] [s4] [s5] [s6]",
    "international": "[Standing rule] Central minimum TOEFL section scores for tests through January 21, 2026: writing 22, speaking 23, reading 21, listening 17. For tests from January 22, 2026: writing 4.5, speaking 4.5, reading 4, listening 4. IELTS minimum is 7; the page gives a Duolingo range of 120-130, so confirm the applicable program threshold if using it. Scores must fall within two years of the semester start. Teaching assistants face a separate spoken-English assessment/training process; an admission waiver does not establish teaching certification. [s4] [s14] [Conflict] A completed UChicago degree taught entirely in English supports a central, case-by-case waiver request. Central instructions require an official transcript or registrar’s English-instruction letter sent directly. The mathematics FAQ describes an undergraduate-English-study exception of three years, creating uncertainty for an applicant whose bachelor’s is still in progress. Obtain written approval and confirm that the portal removes the requirement; use https://go.rutgers.edu/emailgrad for admissions contact. [s3] [s4] [Standing rule] International applicants may be considered for ordinary departmental TA, GA and fellowship funding. Eligibility for each named award may impose additional conditions; a reference to fellowships for US applicants on one page is not evidence that all departmental funding excludes international students. [s2] [s10]",
    "costs": "[Standing rule] USD 70, nonrefundable. Payment follows submission by credit card or e-check; selecting pay later does not make an unpaid application ready for review. [s5] [s6]",
    "size": "University enrollment and mathematics cohort size are not verified in this dossier.",
    "greGeneral": "[Standing rule] Optional; neither the central program listing nor department requires the GRE General Test. [s1] [s2] [s3]",
    "greMath": "[Conflict] Not required, but the department highly recommends the mathematics subject test and describes it as carrying substantial weight. The FAQ also says absence of GRE scores will not hurt an application. These statements are not equivalent to an indifferent or prohibited policy; confirm how the Fall 2027 committee reconciles them. [s1] [s2] [s3]",
    "letters": "[Standing rule] At least three letters; additional letters may be submitted. Choose referees who can evaluate mathematical preparation and potential for doctoral research. [s1] [s2] [s3]",
    "writing": "[Standing rule] A personal statement serving as the statement of purpose is required. Prompt, paraphrased: explain reasons for pursuing doctoral mathematics and choosing Rutgers, mathematical interests, previous research and possible research directions; an explanation of weak grades may be included if useful. Be specific and concise. The public pages give no numerical length, font or spacing limit. Use the live portal to capture the exact final prompt before drafting; a later replacement statement can be uploaded. [s1] [s3] [s5]",
    "warnings": "[Conflict] Resolve the CV required/optional mismatch; GRE-math strong recommendation versus no-disadvantage language; central completed-degree versus departmental English-study waiver wording; unofficial-score review versus central official-score requirements; and divergent stipend and support-duration statements. Current faculty appointments also supersede stale research-group listings: Chi Li is at Johns Hopkins, Stephen Miller is a Rutgers research visitor, and Iwaniec, Beck and Weibel are emeritus. [s1] [s2] [s3] [s4] [s9] [s10] [s11] [s12] [s17] [s18] [s39] [s40]",
    "checks": "[Unresolved] Confirm the application time zone; applicability of the January 1 reference grace date; English waiver while the degree is in progress; GRE-math weighting; CV/statement limits and live short answers; final official-record and commitment dates; the full 2027 funding package; named-award forms; submitted-letter replacement and cross-cycle reuse; and each potential adviser’s availability. No unverified portal-only rule has been filled with a generic assumption. [s1] [s2] [s3] [s4] [s7] [s9] [s11] [s15]",
